WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(smooth music)

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.453
- What's up guys?

00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.030
Michael here to talk about
"South Park", wait what was that?

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.690
Sure.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:13.460
Sorry, we're actually talking about war.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.680
The past few weeks, the
media's been saturated

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.310
with the latest upsetting updates

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.310
about Russia's attack on Ukraine.

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.310
And because we have beating hearts,

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.680
the whole situation has
had us pretty disturbed.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:24.910
It's not that we're strangers

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.100
to hearing about armed
conflict in the news.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.660
Between civil wars, long-term
violent unrest over territory,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.890
and the occasional undeclared American war

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.480
that lasts a goddamn lifetime,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.920
it can start to feel like
state sanctioned violence

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.870
is perpetual, contractable,
and somewhat ubiquitous.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.710
But as we watch the most surprising

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.980
and depressing events of 2022 play out

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.450
we've been wondering,
what is the point of war?

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.840
Why do human beings always
seem to be waging it?

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.040
Is it natural?

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.399
And yes of course, what is it good for?

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:55.910
("War" by Edwin Star plays)
♪ Absolutely nothin' ♪

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.830
So join us on this very
lighthearted journey

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.750
into the deeply violent depths of mankind

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.620
in this Wisecrack Edition on war.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.453
Why?

00:01:05.453 --> 00:01:07.950
The answer to the question why war

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:09.620
might at first seem pretty obvious.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.810
The way most people explain
humanities propensity for war

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:17.030
is that it's inevitable, it's
natural, it just kind of is.

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.780
In fact if we had the production budget

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.770
to man on the street to
question is war natural,

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:24.610
we're pretty sure that one
we'd overwhelmingly hear yes,

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:27.404
and two I'd probably get beaten
up on Hollywood Boulevard.

00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:29.800
Anyway that gut reaction has some

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.820
scientific and philosophical credence.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.500
Most obviously there's the
classic philosophical screen

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.640
from one Thomas Hobbes

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.070
who argued that life is
nasty, brutish, and short,

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.330
and he didn't mean nasty in a good way.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.657
Or as the poet Nas once said.

00:01:43.657 --> 00:01:44.490
("Life's a Bitch" by Nas playing)

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.490
♪ Life's a (beep) and then you die ♪

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:46.720
Other proponents of this view

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.980
that violence amongst
mankind just kinda happens

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.580
would include the likes of William James

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.600
and Sigmund Freud.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.700
And plenty of anthropological scholarship

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.130
bolsters this belief, with
fossilized human bodies

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.000
showing evidence of man on man violence.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.840
Since the 1960's anthropologists

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.390
have used the savanna savanna

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.750
as a surrogate for early humans
and found that they appear

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.180
to really like hitting each other.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.670
Famously a study of the Yanomamö people,

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:13.150
one of the last few remaining
truly hunter-gatherer tribes

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.859
conducted by controversial
anthropologist Napoleon Chagnon,

00:02:16.859 --> 00:02:19.450
found that men who
committed multiple murders

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.880
had more reproductive success,

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.920
I-E acquired more wives
than those who didn't.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.000
He used these findings
to argue that violence

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.030
is a desirable evolutionary
trait in male humans,

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.588
which yeah that tracks

00:02:32.588 --> 00:02:34.570
with every James Bond movie I've seen.

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.930
However many scholars push
back on this idea altogether.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.620
Take the Yanomamö example.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.210
For starters, it's been alleged

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:43.140
that much of the violence Chagnon observed

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:47.277
amongst the Yanomamö was
actually caused by Chagnon

00:02:47.277 --> 00:02:49.410
who handed out machetes and axes

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.440
to encourage the tribe members
to participate in his study.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.640
What's more, anthropologist
Douglas Fry's analysis

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.680
of Chagnon's data finds that
the men who committed murders

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.330
were on average 10 full years
older than the peaceful ones,

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:06.860
giving them way more
opportunity to land a few wives.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:08.909
This is obviously an isolated example,

00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:12.270
but it speaks to a much bigger
epistemological problem,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.180
that is a problem with the
way we acquire knowledge.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.400
According to Fry,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:19.580
western scholars studying
ancient hunter-gatherer cultures

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.060
tend to suffer from a
sort of confirmation bias.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.560
Because we live in a world torn up by war

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.740
where violence is just
part of the landscape,

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.940
it's easy for folks
studying ancient humans

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.830
to find evidence of war everywhere.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.040
Skeletons of cavemen

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.130
that show damage to the skull for instance

00:03:34.130 --> 00:03:35.790
were typically interpreted to be evidence

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.740
that one guy clubbed
the other guy to death,

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.060
thus humans are naturally violent.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.570
But Fry argues that upon closer inspection

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:46.140
some cases like these have
been found to merely indicate

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.640
that the ancient human
was attacked by animals,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.250
or that their skeleton had gone through

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.190
run of the mill archeological decay.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.780
Or take the 9,000 year
old walls of Jericho,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.350
commonly thought of as the first

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:58.340
archeological evidence of war,

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.830
which have also caused some
serious scholarly disagreement.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.070
Some experts now believe
that the walls were built

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.990
to protect the city against flooding

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:07.980
rather than against hostile neighbors.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.870
In his book "Beyond War"
Fry makes a compelling case

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.600
that for the majority of our existence

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.900
war has actually been extremely rare.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.500
He notes that although
the violence of homicide,

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:19.360
feud, and war grab our attention,

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.490
an examination of cross-cultural data

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.940
reveals that people
usually deal with conflicts

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.950
without using any violence at all.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.680
While violence between
individuals, usually male,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.330
seems to have always been around,

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.510
Fry argues that the unmitigated killing

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:33.790
between two or more groups

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:36.700
that is necessary to
constitute capital W war

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.300
is remarkably absent from
most of human history.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:40.880
Scholar John Gowdy adds

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.700
that judging from historical
accounts of hunter-gatherers

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.090
for most of the time humans
have been on the planet

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.630
we have lived in relative harmony

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:49.750
with the natural world
and with each other.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.190
Our minds and cultures evolved
under these conditions.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.250
Evidence suggests that war emerged

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.980
only when human social
order became more complex,

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:01.630
adopting among other things
hierarchy's and horses.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.560
What's more, large scale war
appears to have only existed

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.330
for less than 2,000 years.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:08.840
A mere drop in the bucket for homosapiens.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:12.420
Fry even argues that human
beings have successfully evolved

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:15.350
precisely because
evolution favors restraint

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.340
and limited interpersonal aggression.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.350
In fact, it's one of our strengths.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.430
Human beings are creative
and adaptive enough

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:23.630
to find non-violent solutions to problems,

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:27.490
such as soliciting a friendly
mediator, talking things out,

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.560
or my favorite tactic, simply
running the (beep) away

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.560
from the angry guy holding the club.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:34.300
Now the idea that human beings

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.420
are naturally prone towards
peace is of course contested.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:38.460
But at the very least,

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.120
fry's account throws a
serious shade of doubt

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.940
on the assumption that cavemen
were prone to group violence,

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.520
and that human beings
are basically chimpanzees

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:47.550
with less facial hair.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:49.817
And many scholars agree
with Fry's contention

00:05:49.817 --> 00:05:52.190
that most human societies existed

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:54.750
largely in a state of peace and abundance.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.790
You may be wondering why we're talking

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.200
about ancient hunter-gatherers

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.140
in our purported attempt
to understand modern war,

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.550
but it's really important

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:06.230
that we not take the reality
of warfare for granted.

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.500
That's because assuming that humanity

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:10.440
is naturally war happy

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:15.050
necessarily changes the way we
react to and think about war.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:16.080
As Fry explains,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.660
naturalizing war creates an unfortunate

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:19.950
self fulfilling prophecy.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.190
If war is natural,

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:23.530
then there is little point
in trying to prevent,

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.070
reduce, or abolish it.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.610
It's like the macro version
of the depressing statistic

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.310
that people with a gun in their house

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.350
are more likely to die of homicide.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:32.930
And we'd argue that the assumption

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.200
that war amongst humans is natural

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.590
underpins a lot of the narratives

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.830
we tell ourselves about ourselves.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.350
These days it's easy to
feel like war is inevitable.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:43.680
Scholar James Hillman writes,

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.500
during the 5,600 years of written history

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.770
14,600 wars have been recorded,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.300
two or three wars each
year of human history.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.230
That's pretty (beep) staggering.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:55.790
But if you, like us,

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:58.936
have a strong distaste for
organized human violence,

00:06:58.936 --> 00:07:01.510
it's worth looking to peaceful societies

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.610
no matter how differently
they might be structured

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:05.200
from our own.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:06.910
What makes indiscriminate violence

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:09.420
between two or more
groups of people so rare

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:11.050
until relatively recently?

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:13.530
Arguably the lack of fixed group identity

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.160
plays a pretty big role.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.650
See in ancient hunter-gatherer societies

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.490
if you belonged to one band of people

00:07:19.490 --> 00:07:21.453
and got in a big fight
with your bunk mate,

00:07:21.453 --> 00:07:23.680
you would often simply leave that band

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:24.810
and join a different one.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:26.680
And because lineage was frequently tracked

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:30.190
both matrilineally and patriarch-ally

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:32.390
you would have kin in multiple tribes,

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:34.500
so you had friendly options.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:36.610
What's more, the overlapping flux

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.050
of the state of group membership

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.970
made violence between
groups less likely overall.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:44.180
As Fry explains about the
Aboriginal people of Australia,

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.200
band membership was
neither closed nor static.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.480
Individuals and families
shifted band residence

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.800
over both the short-term
and the long-term.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.390
Virtually every person
had relatives and contacts

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.490
in other groups.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:58.030
These overlapping social
and kinship networks

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:00.160
that spanned the flexible bands

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.400
served to damp inter group hostility.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.900
The idea of floating
between a network of groups

00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.500
at first blush might be
hard for us to relate to.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.130
Most of us forge our identity

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:11.800
largely based on the groups
we permanently belong to,

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.410
like bald dudes with hearts of gold

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.180
and slightly raised cholesterol.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:19.730
And often we identify primarily
by our country of origin.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:23.100
As scholar Benedict
Anderson wrote back in 1873,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:26.040
nation-ness is the most
universally legitimate value

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.700
in the political life of our time.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:28.910
But it's important to note

00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:32.040
that the idea of national
identity is a relatively new one

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:35.190
if you take the grand span of
human history into account.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.880
Some scholars note that medieval elites

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:39.330
may have had a national consciousness

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:41.630
thanks in part to the
influence of the church,

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:43.660
this didn't really reach the masses

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:46.650
and become modern nationalism
until the eighteenth century

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.930
when thanks to enlightenment
and romantic thinking,

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:52.020
nationalism became what
scholar John Hutchinson calls

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:53.420
a surrogate religion.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.370
Whereas previously
borders have been porous,

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.220
the concept of a unified nation

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.600
carried a greater sense of solidarity

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.660
when it emerged in
Europe around this time.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:05.410
Anderson attributes the
rise of European nationalism

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:07.910
in the late 1700's to three things.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:09.220
First the fall of Latin

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:11.360
as Europe's universal religious language,

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.540
accompanied by the rise
of national dialects.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:15.530
Second the death of the divine monarch

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:17.060
in the cultural imagination,

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.070
which (woman screams) required now

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:19.970
precariously positioned rulers

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:21.980
to seek out other forms of legitimacy.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:25.150
And lastly the emergence of
the mass produced newspaper,

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:28.620
which united countrymen in a
shared experience in worldview.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:29.980
These factors coalesce

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.830
to start building more
robust national identities.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:34.640
Now of course there was war

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.770
before there were modern nations.

00:09:36.770 --> 00:09:38.250
Ancient empires didn't form

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:40.350
out of pure fondness for their leaders.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.160
And there were outliers of
large scale long-term warfare

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:45.880
long before the eighteenth century,

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:49.410
such as the hundred years
war which ended in 1453.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.840
See England and France
used it to figure out

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:55.830
who gets to own and be France.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:59.240
Obviously a young Chicago
woman named Emily Cooper

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:00.270
who is in Paris,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:02.080
'cause we're talking
about "Emily in Paris".

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.450
But if anything, this was a
precursor of things to come.

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:07.700
The people involved were
starting to be attuned

00:10:07.700 --> 00:10:09.620
to large collective identities

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.700
that would solidify and
strengthen in future centuries.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:14.610
Anderson refers to a nation

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.850
as an imagined political community,

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.970
because a person in any given country

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:20.560
could never possibly know

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.400
all of their fellow community members.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.420
Creating a nation Anderson explains,

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.400
also forges a deep horizontal comrade-ship

00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:29.180
that is shared amongst its people.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.740
But according to him,
this sense of camaraderie

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:33.850
is not without potential consequence.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.470
He contends that ultimately

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:37.610
it is this fraternity
that makes it possible

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:38.890
over the past two centuries,

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:42.290
for so many millions of
people, not so much to kill,

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:45.500
as willingly to die for
such limited imaginings.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.350
That is perhaps to say

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:50.100
that without our deep national allegiances

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:51.230
it wouldn't be possible

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:54.650
to convince so many people to
go to war in the first place.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.630
To be clear, Anderson's
views are not unopposed.

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.330
And some scholars think the connection

00:10:59.330 --> 00:11:02.490
between nationalism and
war can be overstated.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:03.528
But interestingly enough

00:11:03.528 --> 00:11:07.140
others argue that there's
also a reciprocal relationship

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.130
between war and nationalism.

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:12.100
Scholar John Hutchinson argues
that wars and their aftermath

00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:14.740
only further bolster national identities.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:18.038
That is to say, our nations
might bring us to war

00:11:18.038 --> 00:11:21.340
but war also brings us
closer to our nations.

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:24.000
Or as scholar Charles Tilly says,

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.310
war made the state and the state made war.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:29.380
Now obviously there's nothing wrong

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.240
with being proud of your country of origin

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.550
or with having your
identity deeply tied to it.

00:11:33.550 --> 00:11:36.320
But Fry's research does
substantiate Anderson's argument.

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.070
He found pretty conclusively
that as society trended away

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.590
from loose hierarchy free collectives

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:44.900
and towards governed
organized nation states,

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.780
war became far more
likely across the board.

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:49.820
That's not exactly surprising.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.410
You can't assemble an army

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.670
out of a small band of
loosely connected people,

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.400
but you can destroy the
one ring to rule them all.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:58.560
Militarism necessarily relies

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.700
on butt loads of folks
willing to risk their lives

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.030
for something bigger,

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:05.520
and that something is very often a nation.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:08.040
And that deeply ingrained
sense of national pride

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.320
facilitates the kinds of
wars our ancient ancestors

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:13.760
never would've thought to
wage in the first place.

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:16.620
Of course you need more
than just a complex society

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:19.800
to wage a war, and not
all complex societies do.

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:21.613
Fry determined back in 2006

00:12:21.613 --> 00:12:24.460
that 20 countries in recent history

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:27.090
have gone at least 100
years without a war.

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:29.550
You need one more all important thing

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.340
and it's not a literal enemy.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:34.230
As Hillman writes in his book,
"A Terrible Love of War",

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.010
all along we are speaking
of the idea of an enemy,

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:37.950
a phantom enemy.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.470
It is not the enemy
that is essential to war

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:43.730
and that forces war upon
us, but the imagination.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:46.170
Imagination is the driving force.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:48.340
The way we imagine our enemies then

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:51.150
rather than any reality
based rage or hatred,

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.480
is often the true instigator of war.

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:56.300
We don't talk a lot about
the nature of war today,

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:58.400
which shows the organized battle fields

00:12:58.400 --> 00:12:59.610
of say the Civil War

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.200
where reporters could safely
observe from the sidelines,

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:03.459
but targeted air strikes

00:13:03.459 --> 00:13:06.120
that typically dig into
civilian territory.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.360
And specifically we don't often discuss

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:11.500
how war is actually more
dangerous for regular folks

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.580
than ever before.

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:14.950
As reporter Caroline Nordstrom notes,

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.080
approximately 90 percent of casualties

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:19.570
in modern warfare are civilians.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:21.840
The enemy is no longer enlisted soldiers

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.290
fulfilling duties in the trenches

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:26.730
as we've seen all too glaringly
in the coverage of Ukraine,

00:13:26.730 --> 00:13:29.550
as well as in decades
of American air strikes

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:31.970
in the middle East, just to name a few.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.290
Today it's hard to imagine a war

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.917
that doesn't involve civilian casualties.

00:13:36.917 --> 00:13:39.630
And this can feel pretty
depressing when you're someone

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:43.070
with functionally no
control over geopolitics.

00:13:43.070 --> 00:13:44.620
Fry is ever the optimist.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:47.460
He notes that humans
have survived for so long

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.770
precisely because we're
highly inventive and adaptive.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:53.000
After all, we largely went from living

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:56.150
in tiny bands of foragers
to industrialized cities

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:58.330
in the evolutionary blink of an eye.

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:01.500
And he argues that with
enough political will

00:14:01.500 --> 00:14:02.970
we could use that adaptiveness

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.410
to construct a world without wars.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:08.100
And while that may sound a bit sentimental

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:11.110
we thought we owed you at
least one uplifting thought.

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:12.870
So what do you guys think?

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:16.440
Is war just the inevitable
result of modern civilization?

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:19.020
Would we be better off if
society had never come up

00:14:19.020 --> 00:14:21.120
with alternatives to
hunting and gathering?

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.760
Let us know in the comments.

